Read-aloud, dictation and language-aware speech for the DeepSeek Harness (DSH) web UI.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Install：Install from npm: `dsh plugin --profile web add @lucagiftzek/dsh-voice`, or from GitHub: `dsh plugin --profile web add github:lucagiftzek/dsh-voice`.

```sh
dsh plugin --profile web add @lucagiftzek/dsh-voice

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Alibaba DashScope (`dashscope`)：Alibaba Qwen3-TTS on the pay-as-you-go workspace (ten languages, no Greek). Legacy, off by default.

Residency: International region is Singapore TTS languages: en zh ja ko de fr ru pt es it. STT languages: any. Presets: DashScope (pay as you go). Legacy, off unless `legacy.engines` is on.

Verification: Carried from the 1.x adapter (live in 2026-09); legacy, off unless legacy.engines is on.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Kokoro (native /api/tts) (`tzekos-native`)：A Kokoro-82M server with a native POST /api/tts call, for example behind a relay (CPU is enough).

Residency: Self-hosted: data stays wherever the server runs TTS languages: en. Presets: Kokoro server. Credential: the server key. Only `POST /api/tts` is ever called.

Verification: Verified against the reference Kokoro server and the relay contract (POST /api/tts only, no probes).

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Language detection：1. **Script**: a sentence mostly in Greek (or Cyrillic, Arabic, Hebrew, CJK) is that language.
2. **Words**: Latin-script sentences are scored on stopwords and cues (accents, `-zione`, elisions, `-ing`, contractions) for English and Italian, plus French, German and Spanish when they are enabled. Short sentences (under 4 words) and unsure scores follow their neighbours, so "OK." between Greek sentences stays Greek and an English technical word inside an Italian sentence stays Italian.
3. **Model** (`detection.mode: heuristic+model`, the default): sentences still under `detection.minConfidence`, and Greek sentences holding a run of Latin words, go to the DSH model chosen in Settings (default `mistral-small-latest`, fallback `deepseek-flash`) in ONE batched call per message through the host LLM runtime, within `detection.timeoutMs` (2.5 s). Answers are cached in memory by a hash of the normalised sentence (the text is not stored). On a host without the LLM runtime, on a timeout or an unusable answer, the heuristics stand; detection never delays a read beyond the timeout and never fails one.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Reading：- **Highlight** (`reading.highlight`: `word` default, `sentence`, `off`): the sentence being read is always lit, the word too when timings exist (requested with `/tts?alignment=1`): ElevenLabs character timings, Azure WordBoundary events, Amazon Polly speech marks, Google SSML mark timepoints, Kokoro-FastAPI captions, Cartesia and Acapela word timestamps (the timing badge on each provider row says which), the browser voice's word boundaries, or else a proportional estimate (letters per word plus pauses after commas and full stops, scaled to the real audio length). The highlight uses the CSS Custom Highlight API, so the chat DOM is never changed; it follows the media clock, so pause, resume, skipping and speed changes stay in sync. The sentence in view is scrolled to, unless you scrolled in the last 5 seconds; `prefers-reduced-motion` turns smooth scrolling off. The word highlight is underlined, not colour only.
- **Controls** while reading: stop, pause/resume, next sentence.
- **Auto-read** (`reading.autoRead`: `off` default, `new-replies`, `new-replies-when-dictated`): reads replies that finish while the chat is in view, never its history; q

@lucagiftzek/dsh-voice README · Privacy：- **No audio is stored.** The host keeps nothing: audio and transcripts pass through memory only, temporary files made for conversion are deleted in a `finally`, and logs carry the route, provider, status, character count and duration, never text or audio. The only way audio leaves the page for disk is **Download audio** in the TTS Reader, which you trigger yourself (`privacy.storeAudio` and `privacy.logTranscripts` stay off).
- Language detection sends only the unsure sentences of a message to the DSH model chosen in Settings; its cache holds a hash, not the text.
- **Which providers leave the EU.** Each row shows its residency: the *EU* group (EU company, EU processing) and the *self-hosted* group keep data in the EU or on your machine; *EU region available* means a non-EU company with EU regions (Azure, Google, Amazon, ElevenLabs on its residency plan, OpenAI on its EU project): choose the EU region explicitly, the region is part of the instance and shown in the row; *outside the EU* (DashScope, Cartesia, Fish, and any provider left on its default US endpoint) receives the text or audio you send it; MiMo's Token Plan host is in Amsterdam b

